The verdict is PASS, and it's written to session_plan/eval_task_1.md.

• What changed: /bg kill now really stops the job. Each job's shell runs in its own process group, and kill sends SIGKILL to the whole group. It reuses the existing GroupKill helper from src/tools.rs; that file only had its visibility widened.
• Status after a kill: /bg list shows the real -9 (SIGKILL) exit instead of a placeholder code. A job that exits on its own keeps its own code, e.g. exit 3 → 3.
• #1063 still holds: once the shell exits normally, the group handle is switched off, so a backgrounded grandchild is still left running.
• Tests: I ran the 5 new real-process tests directly and all 5 passed. They cover the shell dying, the grandchild dying, SIGKILL being reported, the near-miss exit code, and the grandchild surviving a normal exit.
• Docs: the "Known gap" line in docs/src/usage/commands.md is replaced with a description of the fixed behaviour, and the ARCHITECTURE.md entry for commands_bg.rs is updated. The remaining #1077 mentions are code comments explaining the fix, not stale pointers.

Three things differ from what the task asked for or change what users see. All are documented:
• SIGKILL only: the task asked for SIGTERM first, then SIGKILL after a grace period. The diff sends SIGKILL straight away so that one group-kill helper serves both places.
• No terminal input: on Linux and macOS, jobs now get empty stdin. A job reading the terminal from its own process group would otherwise be stopped and show as running forever.
• Quitting stops jobs: a job still running when yoyo quits is now killed along with its process group.

Two small races aren't covered by the tests, and I didn't fail on them:
• If /bg kill runs before the job has recorded its process group, only the shell is killed, not its children.
• If a kill lands in the short gap between the shell exiting and the handle being switched off, it could still kill a grandchild that a normal exit would have left alone.
